### What does a DLQ actually do?

A DLQ takes a record the consumer could not process and moves it aside so the main partition keeps flowing. That is genuinely valuable. But notice what it does not do: it does not fix anything, it does not tell anyone, and it does not get the record processed. The failure is relocated, not resolved.

```java
package com.bank.dlq;

public class WhatDlqDoes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("What a DLQ DOES:");
        System.out.println("  + Isolates failing records from the main path");
        System.out.println("  + Prevents a poison pill from blocking the partition");
        System.out.println("  + Preserves the original bytes for later inspection");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("What a DLQ does NOT do:");
        System.out.println("  - Does NOT fix the root cause");
        System.out.println("  - Does NOT alert anyone");
        System.out.println("  - Does NOT get the record processed");
        System.out.println("  - Does NOT classify failures");
        System.out.println("  - Does NOT replay records");
    }
}
```

```text
What a DLQ DOES:
  + Isolates failing records from the main path
  + Prevents a poison pill from blocking the partition
  + Preserves the original bytes for later inspection

What a DLQ does NOT do:
  - Does NOT fix the root cause
  - Does NOT alert anyone
  - Does NOT get the record processed
  - Does NOT classify failures
  - Does NOT replay records
```

### Why is a bare DLQ dangerous?

A DLQ with nothing around it converts a loud failure (a stuck partition someone notices) into a silent failure (a growing pile nobody sees). In a bank, this is worse because lost transactions accumulate invisibly.

```java
package com.bank.dlq;

public class SilentGraveyard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without DLQ:");
        System.out.println("  Partition stuck -> consumer lag spikes -> LOUD");
        System.out.println("  Team notices within minutes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With bare DLQ (no monitoring):");
        System.out.println("  Records go to DLT -> partition flows -> no lag spike");
        System.out.println("  Nobody looks at DLT -> failures pile up -> SILENT");
        System.out.println("  Weeks later: audit finds 5000 unprocessed transfers");
    }
}
```

```text
Without DLQ:
  Partition stuck -> consumer lag spikes -> LOUD
  Team notices within minutes

With bare DLQ (no monitoring):
  Records go to DLT -> partition flows -> no lag spike
  Nobody looks at DLT -> failures pile up -> SILENT
  Weeks later: audit finds 5000 unprocessed transfers
```

### What monitoring do you need?

Track DLQ depth and growth rate, and alert when either crosses a threshold. A sudden spike is a strong incident signal.

```java
package com.bank.monitoring;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Gauge;

public class DlqMonitoring {
    // Expose DLT lag as a Prometheus metric
    public static void registerMetric(MeterRegistry registry, long dltLag) {
        Gauge.builder("kafka.dlt.lag", () -> dltLag)
            .tag("topic", "transfers.DLT")
            .description("Dead letter topic lag")
            .register(registry);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DLQ monitoring rules:");
        System.out.println("  Alert if DLT depth > 0         (any failure)");
        System.out.println("  Page if DLT growth > 100/min   (dependency down)");
        System.out.println("  Dashboard: DLT depth per topic");
        System.out.println("  Route alert to OWNING team, not a shared channel");
    }
}
```

```text
DLQ monitoring rules:
  Alert if DLT depth > 0         (any failure)
  Page if DLT growth > 100/min   (dependency down)
  Dashboard: DLT depth per topic
  Route alert to OWNING team, not a shared channel
```

### How do you classify root causes?

```java
package com.bank.classification;

public class DlqClassification {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Root-cause classification:");
        System.out.println("  TRANSIENT : database timeout, service 503");
        System.out.println("    Action  : replay once dependency recovers");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  PERMANENT : validation error, business rule rejection");
        System.out.println("    Action  : fix code or data, then replay or discard");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  POISON    : undeserializable bytes, schema mismatch");
        System.out.println("    Action  : fix producer or schema, discard bad records");
    }
}
```

```text
Root-cause classification:
  TRANSIENT : database timeout, service 503
    Action  : replay once dependency recovers

  PERMANENT : validation error, business rule rejection
    Action  : fix code or data, then replay or discard

  POISON    : undeserializable bytes, schema mismatch
    Action  : fix producer or schema, discard bad records
```

### What does a replay procedure look like?

A written, tested redrive procedure avoids improvising under pressure.

```java
package com.bank.runbook;

public class RedriveRunbook {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Redrive runbook:");
        System.out.println("  1. Verify fix is deployed and tested");
        System.out.println("  2. Inspect DLT records, classify each");
        System.out.println("  3. Replay transient failures to reprocessing topic");
        System.out.println("  4. Throttle at 50 records/second");
        System.out.println("  5. Monitor consumer success rate during replay");
        System.out.println("  6. Discard permanent failures with audit trail");
        System.out.println("  7. Verify DLT drained, close incident");
    }
}
```

```text
Redrive runbook:
  1. Verify fix is deployed and tested
  2. Inspect DLT records, classify each
  3. Replay transient failures to reprocessing topic
  4. Throttle at 50 records/second
  5. Monitor consumer success rate during replay
  6. Discard permanent failures with audit trail
  7. Verify DLT drained, close incident
```

### Why is ownership critical?

A shared responsibility is nobody's responsibility. Without a named team and a service-level agreement, the DLQ grows indefinitely.

```java
package com.bank.ownership;

public class DlqOwnership {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DLQ ownership model:");
        System.out.println("  Topic: transfers.DLT");
        System.out.println("  Owner: Settlement team");
        System.out.println("  SLA  : DLT drained within 4 hours of alert");
        System.out.println("  Escalation: if not drained in 4h, page lead");
    }
}
```

```text
DLQ ownership model:
  Topic: transfers.DLT
  Owner: Settlement team
  SLA  : DLT drained within 4 hours of alert
  Escalation: if not drained in 4h, page lead
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class DlqMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: enable DLT and walk away
        System.out.println("WRONG: DLT enabled, no monitoring, no owner");
        System.out.println("  Result: silent graveyard of lost transactions");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: DLT + monitoring + classification + ownership
        System.out.println("CORRECT: DLT as incident-response mechanism");
        System.out.println("  Alerts, runbooks, triage, ownership, metrics");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: DLT enabled, no monitoring, no owner
  Result: silent graveyard of lost transactions

CORRECT: DLT as incident-response mechanism
  Alerts, runbooks, triage, ownership, metrics
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

When records land in the DLQ, an incident has effectively occurred even if nothing crashed, because business events are not being processed. Treating the DLQ as a config option gives a false sense of safety. Treating it as incident response means alerts, runbooks, triage, ownership, and metrics, exactly like any other production incident path.
